statement:
Do there exist two bipartite states, each with zero two-way distillable entanglement, whose tensor product has positive two-way distillable entanglement?
Let $\rho_{AB}$ and $\sigma_{A'B'}$ be states on arbitrary finite-dimensional bipartite Hilbert spaces. For any bipartite state $\omega$, let $D_{\leftrightarrow}(\omega)$ denote its asymptotic distillable entanglement, in maximally entangled qubit pairs per copy, under local quantum operations and unlimited two-way classical communication (LOCC). When the two states are combined, Alice controls $AA'$ and Bob controls $BB'$, and the distillable entanglement of the product is taken across the cut $AA':BB'$. No additional entangled state, catalyst, or extra separated party is available. The question is whether some pair satisfies
\begin{equation}
 D_{\leftrightarrow}(\rho_{AB})=D_{\leftrightarrow}(\sigma_{A'B'})=0,
 \qquad
 D_{\leftrightarrow}(\rho_{AB}\otimes\sigma_{A'B'})>0.
 \label{eq:05aa-superactivation}
\end{equation}

progress:
Write $T_B$ for partial transposition on Bob's system; a state $\omega$ is PPT when $\omega^{T_B}\geq0$ and NPT otherwise. Horodecki, Horodecki, and Horodecki proved that PPT states have $D_\leftrightarrow=0$ \sourcecite{ref:05aa-horodecki}{HHH98}. Since
\begin{equation}
 (\rho_{AB}\otimes\sigma_{A'B'})^{T_{BB'}}=\rho_{AB}^{T_B}\otimes\sigma_{A'B'}^{T_{B'}}\geq0
 \label{eq:05aa-ppt-closure}
\end{equation}
whenever both factors are PPT, any pair satisfying Eq.~\eqref{eq:05aa-superactivation} contains an NPT state with zero $D_\leftrightarrow$.

Vollbrecht and Wolf constructed, for each local dimension $d$, PPT bound entangled states that make every entangled $d\times d$ Werner state one-copy distillable when adjoined to it. Because every NPT state can be converted by LOCC into an entangled Werner state, every NPT state $\omega$ has a finite-dimensional PPT activator $\tau$ such that $\omega\otimes\tau$ satisfies Eq.~\eqref{eq:05aa-criterion} with $n=1$ \sourcecite{ref:05aa-vollbrecht-wolf}{VW02}. An NPT state $\omega$ with $D_\leftrightarrow(\omega)=0$ and such an activator therefore satisfy Eq.~\eqref{eq:05aa-superactivation}. Together with Eq.~\eqref{eq:05aa-ppt-closure}, this gives the equivalence
\begin{equation}
 \exists\,\rho,\sigma:\ D_\leftrightarrow(\rho)=D_\leftrightarrow(\sigma)=0<D_\leftrightarrow(\rho\otimes\sigma)
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 \exists\,\omega:\ \omega^{T_B}\not\geq0,\ D_\leftrightarrow(\omega)=0.
 \label{eq:05aa-equivalence}
\end{equation}
Thus the existence question is equivalent to the existence of NPT bound entanglement, not merely related to it \sourcecite{ref:05aa-horodecki}{HHH98}, \sourcecite{ref:05aa-vollbrecht-wolf}{VW02}.

A principal candidate family is formed by the Werner states
\begin{equation}
 \omega_{d,\beta}:=\frac{I_{d^2}+\beta F_d}{d^2+\beta d},
 \qquad
 F_d|i\rangle|j\rangle:=|j\rangle|i\rangle,
 \qquad
 d\geq3,\quad -\frac12\leq\beta<-\frac1d.
 \label{eq:05aa-werner}
\end{equation}
On the interval in Eq.~\eqref{eq:05aa-werner} these states are NPT but not one-copy distillable, by Theorem~2 of DiVincenzo et al.\ in a different parametrization. Their reduction of every NPT state to an NPT Werner state shows that NPT bound entanglement exists if and only if some state in Eq.~\eqref{eq:05aa-werner} has $D_\leftrightarrow=0$ \sourcecite{ref:05aa-divincenzo-et-al}{DSS+00}. Theorem~1.1 of Fu, Gao, and Park's July 2026 preprint proves that $\omega_{d,\beta}$ is two-copy undistillable if and only if $-1/2\leq\beta\leq1$, for every $d\geq2$. Hence the whole interval in Eq.~\eqref{eq:05aa-werner} is two-copy undistillable, but the criterion in Eq.~\eqref{eq:05aa-criterion} remains undecided there for $n\geq3$ \sourcecite{ref:05aa-fu-gao-park}{FGP26}.

Shor, Smolin, and Terhal gave an explicit candidate pair. A $3\times3$ PPT bound entangled state $\rho_{\mathrm{Pent}}$, built from an unextendible product basis, makes $\omega_{3,\beta}\otimes\rho_{\mathrm{Pent}}$ one-copy distillable for
\begin{equation}
 -\frac12\leq\beta<-\frac{7-\sqrt5}{10},
 \label{eq:05aa-pent-activation}
\end{equation}
which is their range $2\leq\lambda<(10\sqrt5-20)/(17\sqrt5-37)$ rewritten with $\beta=-(\lambda+1)/(3\lambda)$. Consequently, zero $D_\leftrightarrow$ for any single $\omega_{3,\beta}$ in the interval of Eq.~\eqref{eq:05aa-pent-activation} would give an explicit pair satisfying Eq.~\eqref{eq:05aa-superactivation} \sourcecite{ref:05aa-shor-smolin-terhal}{SST01}.
